AB  - Performance prediction is a crucial aspect of ensuring optimal facility operation and maintenance across diverse fields. Although deep learning algorithms have found many applications in several industries, their applicability in tabular data mining that integrates multiple parameter types has remained somewhat limited. To address this gap, this research proposes the introduction of an integrated learning model premised on practical business scenarios, featuring data cleaning, and well-grounded feature engineering methods. Specifically, the study employs the LightGBM and Tabnet fusion model to overcome inefficiencies associated with traditional integrated learning techniques like XGBoost and GBDT. Furthermore, a recursive feature elimination approach is implemented to reduce redundancy during model construction, culminating in the improved online performance prediction. The model developed in this study has demonstrated a 32.66% reduction in prediction deviation compared to traditional manual prediction methods in simulations on large laser facilities. These findings underscore the significance of this integrated learning model in enhancing predictive accuracy, which is a critical component of optimal facility management.
