AB  - This study focuses on analyzing vibration signals from pumps in water treatment plants to detect abnormal operating conditions during real-time operation. The rotary motion of the impeller in conventional centrifugal pumps induces vibrations throughout the pump body. These vibration signals can contain valuable information, including indicators of faults in the impeller or flow channels. This study aims to identify such faults by analyzing the signals in the frequency domain. Real-time vibration data from the pump body were transformed into time-frequency representations using the short-time Fourier transform. Cluster analysis was then applied to these representations to identify potential anomalies. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of an unsupervised, data-driven approach for predictive maintenance of equipment in water treatment plant facilities.
