AB  - Internal damage in pavement structures caused by cyclic traffic loading leads to a progressive decline in load-bearing capacity and overall service life. However, accurately assessing and predicting the in-situ damage state of asphalt pavements remains a significant challenge. This study proposes an approach to inversely quantify cumulative internal damage over pavement service life and to predict optimal maintenance timing based on a damage evolution model. A finite element model updating (FEMU) framework was proposed and integrated with artificial intelligence algorithms to compute the internal damage, i.e. damage density, and layer moduli for 28 pavement sections from the Long-Term Pavement Performance (LTPP) database. A three-parameter damage density evolution model was applied to fit the damage evolution across different sections. The computational results revealed three distinct types of damage evolution trends. Additionally, this study highlights the nonlinear relationship between damage density and surface cracking. Building on this relationship, the damage progression was categorized into three distinct phases in terms of internal damage severity. Finally, the maintenance point for each pavement section was predicted based on the damage evolution curve and associated model. The predicted maintenance point, where the damage density reaches 1 (indicating failure of pavement) in damage evolution curve, exhibited strong correlation with the actual maintenance points recorded in the LTPP database.
