TI  - Predictive maintenance for PV systems in island communities: Ensuring energy reliability and efficiency
T2  - INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE CISBAT 2025
LA  - English
CP  - 2025 International Scientific Conference on the Built Environment in Transition-CISBAT
AB  - This research addresses the unique challenges faced by island communities in maintaining and operating renewable energy systems, particularly photovoltaic (PV) panels, which are crucial for energy autonomy. These communities, often characterised by their remoteness and distinct environmental conditions, frequently experience energy grid instabilities due to the intermittency of solar power generation. Furthermore, the lack of local technical support highlights the need for advanced automated solutions to ensure energy system reliability and efficiency. This research aims to enhance the role of buildings in island energy systems by improving their capacity to generate and efficiently utilise energy through the deployment of machine learning-based predictive maintenance strategies for PV panels. By developing an algorithm that proactively identifies when PV panels are dirty or experiencing performance degradation, the project seeks to optimise energy production while minimising downtime and maintenance costs. The algorithm was tested in data collected from a pilot site in the Aran Islands, Ireland. The results of the study demonstrated a correlation between dirt accumulation and reduced panel efficiency, successfully detecting soiling-related efficiency issues when losses were around 3.8%. Moreover, the model identified both gradual and sudden faults. Finally, an experimental setup is proposed to improve data collection for algorithm training in a controlled environment, which will help further improve the detection models.
