AB  - Abrasion resistance (AR) is a critical indicator of the durability of concrete, directly influencing the safety of building operation and maintenance, particularly for hydraulic engineering construction. In this study, the mechanism of abrasion deterioration is introduced, including the causes of abrasion formation and the types of abrasion damage. The common methods of testing the AR of concrete and evaluation parameters based on different testing methods are summarized and reviewed. Besides, the effects of hydraulic parameters, water-cement ratio, aggregate, modification materials, and supplementary cementitious materials on the abrasion performance of concrete are summarized. The reduction of water-cement ratio and the utilization of wearresistant aggregate are beneficial to the AR of concrete. Fiber is added to concrete to improve the AR of concrete, it is worth noting that the volume fraction of fiber cannot exceed 2.5 %. NanoSiO2 and graphene oxide have been proven they significantly promote the AR of concrete. Replacing cement with supplementary cementitious materials reduces the amount of cement used and may enhance the AR of concrete. However, it is highly dependent on the type and content of additives. According to these factors, crucial approaches are identified for promoting the AR of concrete in severe conditions. It also summarizes and reviews the relevant models used to forecast the AR of concrete, containing empirical formulas, semi-empirical formulas, regression analysis, and machine learning models. Machine learning models have higher prediction accuracy compared to other models, but they require a large database to support them. This study aims to facilitate a deep understanding of the abrasion behavior of concrete, contributing to the development of building materials.
