AB  - Traditional methods of visual inspection and nondestructive testing for civil infrastructure face challenges, including inspector subjectivity and the need for instrument calibration. Structural health monitoring (SHM) is an emerging alternative that enables continuous and cost-effective maintenance. This study presents a digital twin (DT) framework that leverages Internetof-Things technology, providing real-time SHM and remote visualization of public infrastructure. The proposed system delivers a scalable DT solution that integrates drone-enabled modeling, LoRaWAN-based microstrain sensing, and a cloud-based visualization platform. A high-resolution 3D building information model of a floodway facility is created using LiDAR, with sensor nodes connected to the ThingsBoard dashboard interface. The performance and scalability of the proposed system are validated through full-scale deployment. The successful demonstration of system performance under field conditions, drone-based modeling, and real-time data stream visualization is shown using a full-scale floodway facility. The developed DT framework holds potential for SHM application across diverse infrastructure assets and can be extended towards environmental sensing and AI-driven predictive maintenance.
