AB  - Leveraging digital technologies is a key priority in promoting a shift toward more sustainable, resilient, and efficient transport infrastructure. A significant paradigm shift is needed, from building new infrastructure to digitally managing and maintaining existing transport networks. In this context, implementing Pavement Management Systems (PMS) plays a crucial role. PMS must integrate accurate data collection on structural integrity, traffic loads, and environmental conditions to support timely and informed decision-making, ultimately enhancing infrastructure resilience. Recent technological advances, such as Digital Twins (DTs), offer great potential for PMS by enabling a predictive approach to road maintenance. A DT integrates IoT systems, simulation models, cloud computing, and artificial intelligence (AI) to provide a comprehensive, consistent representation of physical infrastructure, facilitating monitoring, analysis, and optimization throughout its lifecycle. The effectiveness of a DT depends on accurate and continuous data exchange between the physical and virtual counterparts, requiring advanced sensing and non-destructive technologies. Traditional sensors, such as strain gauges, optical fibers, and piezoelectric sensors, often face limitations related to durability and compatibility with asphalt pavements. A promising alternative is the development of innovative asphalt materials with self-sensing capabilities. This paper presents a laboratory proof of concept for a DT designed to monitor the structural condition of road pavements and support predictive maintenance. The proposed IoT system leverages an innovative self-sensing approach in which asphalt mixtures modified with carbon fibers (CFs) function as intrinsic sensors. A tailored DT architecture has been developed to ensure efficient data acquisition, processing, storage, analysis, and visualization using dedicated software and cloud-based applications. The concept was validated in a laboratory environment using small-scale asphalt pavement models subjected to controlled fatigue deterioration. Results demonstrate the potential of the self-sensing material and the DT framework, laying the foundation for future scalable and cost-effective real-world applications.
