KW  - magnetostrictive sensor
KW  - EFFICIENCY IMPROVEMENT
AB  - Rotor bar breakage is a critical failure in large induction motors, where unscheduled shutdowns can lead to costs far exceeding the value of the equipment. Conventional diagnostic methods generally rely on frequency domain analysis, which can overlay faults under varying loads. This study presented a diagnostic approach using a magnetic field sensor based on a fiber Bragg grating (FBG) embedded in a magnetostrictive Terfenol-D/polymer composite with oriented magnetic domains. Designed for environments up to 150 degrees C, the sensor was developed to operate within the tight mechanical tolerances of the motor air gap. Unlike traditional techniques, the fault detection strategy focuses primarily on identifying magnetic flux asymmetries in the time domain. Experimental validation was performed on a 4-pole motor, demonstrating that bar breaks induce periodic impulsive transients in the stray magnetic field. These anomalies were identified using the Jarque-Bera (JB) normality test, which functioned as a diagnostic mechanism, detecting deviations from the Gaussian behavior. Integrating this statistical verdict with a threshold and a rotational periodicity filter ensures high diagnostic specificity, making it possible that no false positives were observed in the present set of experimental data. The results confirm that this time-domain approach provides a robust real-time monitoring solution, particularly suitable for predictive maintenance of motors in industrial facilities such as oil refineries.
