TI  - An integrated deep learning and virtual reality framework for automated, remote thermal inspection of buildings
T2  - HEALTH MONITORING OF STRUCTURAL AND BIOLOGICAL SYSTEMS XX
LA  - English
CP  - 2026 Conference on Health Monitoring of Structural and Biological Systems
KW  - Building envelope inspection
KW  - deep learning
KW  - thermal imaging
KW  - 3D reconstruction
KW  - unmanned aerial vehicles
KW  - virtual reality
KW  - YOLO
AB  - Traditional building envelope inspection methods are labor-intensive, subjective, and limited to physically accessible areas. This work presents an integrated framework combining UAV-based thermal imaging, deep learning, and virtual reality (VR) for comprehensive and automated building envelope diagnostics. A DJI Matrice 200 UAV equipped with a DJI Zenmuse XT2 thermal camera captured infrared imagery of university campus buildings under controlled post-sunset conditions at ambient temperatures below -4 degrees C. The acquired thermal images were preprocessed in FLIR Thermal Studio and reconstructed into three-dimensional (3D) thermal point clouds using structure-from-motion photogrammetry in Agisoft Metashape. Screenshots of the resulting 3D models were annotated and used as the original training dataset, further expanded through geometric transformations including flipping, rotation, and scaling. A YOLOv8 architecture was trained on this dataset to detect six thermal signature classes: doors, windows, damaged doors, damaged windows, poor insulation areas, and air vents. To further address thermal data scarcity, a CycleGAN-based augmentation strategy was developed to generate synthetic thermal images from RGB building photographs, with high-quality generated images added to the training set. Model performance was evaluated before and after CycleGAN augmentation, with both models subsequently tested on a fourth building excluded from training to assess cross-building generalization. Preliminary results demonstrate a bounding box Mean Average Precision at 50% Intersection over Union (mAP@50) of 84.3% on the baseline dataset, improving to 89.3% following CycleGAN augmentation, with the SGD optimizer at batch size 16 delivering optimal detection performance. The trained model was converted to ONNX format and integrated into a Unity-based VR environment deployed on Meta Quest 3 headsets, where detected thermal anomalies are rendered as 3D bounding boxes overlaid on the thermal point cloud, enabling remote, quantitative building inspection without physical site access.
