AB  - In plasma and fusion research facilities such as tokamaks, maintenance, inspection, and diagnostic operations are performed under extreme environmental conditions that make direct human intervention impractical and unsafe. The regions surrounding the vacuum vessel are exposed to intense neutron and gamma radiation, high magnetic fields, and elevated temperatures during and after plasma discharges. Furthermore, the presence of activated components and vacuum integrity constraints prohibits manual access even during shutdown periods. These factors necessitate the deployment of advanced remote handling (RH) systems, which enable precise manipulation, inspection, and replacement of in vessel components such as divertor cassettes, diagnostics, and first wall modules without compromising personnel safety or vacuum conditions. However, traditional RH operations face challenges related to limited situational awareness, delayed feedback, and lack of predictive control. These challenges hinder precise manipulation and increase operational risks. To address these challenges, this work presents a real-time synchronized, operational digital twin framework for control and monitoring of RH systems in fusion devices such as tokamaks. The proposed architecture establishes a high fidelity virtual replica synchronized with the physical system using UDP based Ethernet communication. The framework has been implemented and validated on two subsystems namely a Dual Arm Manipulator (DAM) and a Vacuum Automation System (VAS) both employing servo drives. The system provides a scalable platform for future extensions such as predictive maintenance, fault detection, and intelligent control in fusion remote-handling operations.
