AB  - Augmented reality (AR) is evolving as an onsite visualization tool that can facilitate the expedited retrieval and enhanced comprehension of design information in field settings. However, traditional approaches, such as marker-based and markerless AR, either require additional effort for marker installation or face computational challenges in recognizing reference targets stemming from the self-similarity of indoor environments. By leveraging the benefits offered by both approaches, this study investigates the suitability of using existing building elements with known spatial coordinates in a virtual model (e.g., BIM) as reference targets for AR implementations. Experiments were conducted to assess the performance of this approach using six building elements with diverse geometries and dimensions as reference targets across several distinct operational scenarios, including a variety of distances and viewing angles. The experimental results indicated mean errors of 0.315 m and 4.801 degrees in camera-pose estimation, suggesting that building elements can serve effectively as natural markers under optimal working conditions. These conditions include capturing images from the frontal perspective, and maintaining distances no greater than approximately twice the longest dimension of the target reference object. These findings imply that existing building elements in indoor environments have the potential to be used as reference targets for AR implementation.
