AB  - The manufacturing sector commonly employs traditional systems for predicting tool wear states, which rely on one-dimensional numerical inputs, in order to mitigate the issues of escalating costs and reduced efficiency that stem from excessive tool wear. However, these models face drawbacks such as high computational demands and limited generalization capabilities. To overcome these challenges, this study introduces a novel method for predicting tool wear states, which leverages the Gramian Angular Field-Markov Transition Field-AlexNet (GAF-MTF-AlexNet) architecture. Initially, the vibration signal's envelope spectrum is mapped into a two-dimensional image through the application of the GAF and the MTF transformations. Subsequently, the generated image is horizontally stitched to form a comprehensive dataset. The dataset is then fed into the customized AlexNet model for the purpose of prediction. Following meticulous research and optimization, this method enhances signal visualization and allows deep learning algorithms to extract signal features more effectively, resulting in a significant improvement in accuracy to 96.83%. This precision far surpasses that of conventional one-dimensional data input approaches, thus validating the efficacy of the measures implemented. The empirical results demonstrate that the method is proficient in predicting the transition of tools across diverse wear states. Consequently, this approach provides a robust technical foundation for the manufacturing industry to reduce production costs, improve operational efficiency, and facilitate predictive maintenance practices.
