AB  - In response to rising energy demand and the depletion of conventional resources, offshore oil and gas is increasingly pursuing subsea production architectures that leverage advanced sensing, control, robotics, and data-driven decision support. This review provides a comprehensive, metrics-first analysis of autonomous subsea processing systems that shift critical functions from topside facilities to the seabed - separation, boosting/ pumping, compression, heat exchange, and control - to improve efficiency, reduce lifecycle costs, and mitigate environmental impacts under harsh deepwater conditions. Synthesizing the open literature and selected industry evidence, we consolidate the sustainability metrics most relevant to autonomy-enabled subsea systems: energy intensity (e.g., kWh per produced barrel of oil equivalent), operational greenhouse-gas intensity (kgCO2e per boe, including power supply and flaring where reported), and embodied emissions associated with subsea equipment and power/control infrastructure. We then explain how autonomy changes these outcomes through distinct levers: closed-loop set-point optimization of pumps and compressors; improved flow assurance via multiphysics modeling and real-time condition monitoring; reduced chemical use, downtime, and shutdowns through predictive maintenance; and fewer offshore interventions and logistics emissions enabled by remote operations and subsea robotics. The article's core contribution is an integrated framework linking: (i) subsea hardware choices (heat exchangers, gas-liquid separation, pumps, compressors, and control systems) with comparative analysis against topside systems as a baseline; (ii) AI-enabled digital twins for behavior prediction in dynamic environments; and (iii) multiobjective optimization methods that balance economic performance, environmental sustainability, and operational reliability. Finally, we highlight gaps in consistent CO2e and embodied-carbon boundaries, scarcity of public field datasets for validation, and the need for safety- and cybersecurity-aware benchmarks for AI-in-the-loop control, outlining priorities for progressing toward resilient, fully autonomous subsea processing.
