TI  - A digital twin framework for damage detection and localization of self-sensed cured-in-place underground pipelines
T2  - ENGINEERING STRUCTURES
LA  - English
KW  - Distributed fiber optic sensing
KW  - Finite element analysis
KW  - Cured-in-place pipe
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Structural health monitoring
AB  - As the underground pipelines deteriorate with age, their structural vulnerability increases. Cured-in-place pipe (CIPP) lining is an emerging trenchless technology used to rehabilitate deteriorated pipes. However, the buckling behavior of CIPP liner-rehabilitated pipes is insufficiently investigated. This study presents an integrated framework for real-time structural health monitoring and damage detection for underground pipelines by leveraging the knowledge of distributed fiber optic sensors (DFOS), finite element analysis (FEA), deep learning (DL) algorithms, and digital twin. The study experimentally and computationally investigates the structural behavior of a single steel substrate and a CIPP-rehabilitated steel substrate under eccentric compression conditions. The application of DFOS enables the self-sensing function of the pipelines with high-resolution, continuous strain measurement. The finite element model and DL-based strain field correlation are employed to complement the function of DFOS from strain field monitoring to structural deformation assessment, facilitating the development of virtual replicates of self-sensed pipes. The study further explores the location and severity of structural damage through numerical and theoretical strain analysis, enabling the detection of minor but critical defects. The developed digital twin framework enables real-time structural visualization and enhances structural damage assessment. By accurately identifying the early-stage plastic region of deformation and quantifying crack severity, the framework supports predictive maintenance and improves pipeline reliability. This proactive approach improves the safety of underground pipelines, extends their service life, and reduces maintenance costs, demonstrating its practical potential for long-term pipeline infrastructure management.
