KW  - AUTOMATION
KW  - TRUST
KW  - PERSPECTIVE
KW  - MANAGEMENT
KW  - DESIGN
KW  - BIAS
AB  - For decades, Lean manufacturing has been the preeminent framework for attaining operational excellence, facilitating significant enhancements through the methodical eradication of waste in production processes. The ideas have been utilized to enhance the performance of human operators and, independently, to increase the efficiency of automated technology. The emergence of advanced Artificial Intelligence (AI) in manufacturing, encompassing predictive maintenance algorithms and collaborating robots (cobots), introduces a novel dynamic that contests Lean tools and methodology. The fundamental inquiry has shifted from "What can we automate?" to "How can we establish a system in which humans and AI collaborate to accomplish more than either could independently?" This study contends that the dominant perception of AI as a mechanism for automation (i.e., substituting human labor) is restrictive. It signifies an inability to acknowledge the potential for a more profound, synergistic partnership. When AI is utilized just to automate jobs formerly executed by humans, the optimal outcome is machine efficiency. The most unfavorable outcome is a rigid system devoid of the adaptability, contextual insight, and innovative problem-solving capabilities of the human it displaced. To fill these gaps, we introduce the Human-AI Symbiotic Optimization framework. This framework extends the core philosophy of Lean to the domain of human-AI interaction. It re-frames the objective from optimizing individual components (the human or the AI) to optimizing the interaction between them. This extended paper provides a detailed, multi-phase implementation methodology, a framework for organizational change management grounded in established theories like the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), deeper mathematical models for measuring symbiosis, and adaptations of additional Lean tools. By adapting classic Lean tools like Value Stream Mapping and the "Respect for People" principle for the human-AI context, this research provides a theoretical foundation and a practical roadmap for manufacturers to build truly symbiotic, high-performing production systems.
